<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · ideology</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neoliberalism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938 CE</a> – present</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neoliberalism is a free-market ideology named at a 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> colloquium and spread by the Mont Pelerin Society. Its policies were widely applied from the 1970s, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> under Pinochet, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and World Bank.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…several: European secularisation and the loss of a voting base, the death of communism and the rise of <b>neoliberalism</b>, the 2008 financial crisis, and pressure from immigration and populism, which leaves the movement torn between the right's call for restrictions, business calls for an open labour market and…</blockquote><a href="/ideology/christian-democracy">Christian democracy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Right-libertarians such as Rothbard and Hoppe argue that libertarian socialism’s rejection of private property in the means of production relies on coercion, and the anarchist scholar Simon Critchley calls contemporary <b>neoliberalism</b> a “pseudo-libertarianism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/libertarianism">Libertarianism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li></ul></section>
